tnews in montana was always very high i  mean marijuana was ubiquitous in montana  it still is medical marijuana didn't  change it it didn't change the culture  of montana at its core hi i'm anthony  fisher for recent TV and I'm sitting  with Rebecca Richmond Cohen the  filmmaker behind the new documentary  code of the West Rebecca thanks for  talking with us thanks for having me can  you briefly describe the plot of co2 the  West so we set out to make a film about  the legislative process in Montana and  we wanted to follow the public debate  over medical marijuana laws medical  marijuana was legalized in 2004 in  Montana and it was a quiet small program  and then in about 2010 it became very  visible very quickly and because many  communities in Montana didn't even have  the power to create basic zoning laws  flashy dispensaries some opened  kitty-corner from junior high schools  with advertising that looked like it was  targeted children now the interesting  thing is that by a study that was  designed by the Centers for Disease  Control so the federal government it  showed that teen use actually decreased  by every measure steadily since 2004  when Montana first legalized so the  people who are organizing the repeal  movement stood up in front of the state  legislature and made claims that teen  use had increased fourfold and this  information was simply not correct I  think there was a lot of fear I think  there was a lot of misinformation and  the opposition picked up on it the fear  and misinformation was fueled in no  small part by statewide raids that were  conducted by the federal government can  you describe what the code of the West  that is the title of your film what does  that refer to  so the code of the West was another bill  that we followed through the Montana  State Legislature and it was a bill that  would codify the cowboy code of ethics  as the official state code of ethics and  we thought it was a great metaphor about  the sort of anxiety that was also  revealed in the medical marijuana debate  and anxiety that was really about the  identity of Montana and it seemed like  both the people who were pushing to  repeal the medical marijuana law and the  people who were pushing to codify the  cowboy code of ethics had some sort of  nostalgia for a past that was really  imagined and never existed tnews and  Monty  was always very high I mean marijuana  was ubiquitous in Montana it still is  medical marijuana didn't change it it  didn't change the culture of Montana at  its core so there were all these very  strange and superficial debates that  seemed to miss what was really going on  you know as filmmakers it was a great  gift to make to make a film in Montana  where we could really make a character  out of the landscape and this gave us a  sort of lyrical way to explore this  metaphor speaking of characters the  former governor of Montana who's  depicted in your film taking a branding  iron to represent his veto of the  medical marijuana repeal bill can you  talk a little bit about him yeah the  state legislature moved to repeal the  medical marijuana law and Governor Brian  Schweitzer moved to protect the will of  the people and veto to the repeal  literally with a hot stove and a  branding iron on the front steps of the  State Capitol  and it's these these sorts of moments as  a documentarian when you think wow you  know I actually couldn't have scripted  this reality is stranger than fiction  sometimes and so he's a Democratic  governor in a largely Republican state  and he was very popular at the time he  left office was his veto popular so it  depends on how you ask the question  Montanans were very frustrated with the  implementation of the medical marijuana  law and so if you asked the question to  most Montanans would you rather keep the  law as it is a repeal it most Montanans  would in fact have said we prefer repeal  now if you rephrase the question and  said would you prefer regulations and  reform and keeping the medical marijuana  program versus repeal then a much larger  percentage seventy something percent of  Montanans would have would have  preferred just to fix the law where does  the issue stand now yeah well in some  ways we made a strange film about an  outlier state most states are moving  forward in implementing sensible  marijuana policy reforms Montana was one  state that moved backwards but it's also  a really important story to tell and I  think in many ways even more important  to tell now because there's a way to do  this wrong and there's a way to do this  better and one of the wonderful things  about allowing States to be the  laboratories of experimentation is to  figure out what we can learn from the  states that have done it well from  states like New Mexico and Maine where  patients do have access to medical  marijuana  yet there isn't an intense backlash and  also to consider that the federal  government at any moment could come in  and shut this whole experiment down and  that the Obama administration hasn't  been responsive about their plans for  responding to the legalization efforts  in Washington and Colorado and I think  it's very telling that two years ago the  Obama administration spoke out against  the legalization effort in California  yet this November they said nothing  about the the votes in Washington and  Colorado and I think it's also equally  telling that more people in Colorado  voted for marijuana than they voted for  Barack Obama and so I think it's a real  opportunity for the administration to  re-examine our draconian drug policies  their failures and to think through a  more sensible way to govern this thank  you so much Rebecca Richmond : the film  is ko to the West for reason TV i'm  anthony fisher
 

